---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="A 1965 Schrøder Reynolds 531 racer with Nervex lugs, Campagnolo Valentino, Balilla brakes and documented workshop provenance."/><meta property="og:type" content="article"/><meta property="og:title" content="Schrøder 1965 · The Evolution of Bikes"/><meta property="og:description" content="A Copenhagen-built racer whose frame number, first owner and delivery date survive in the Schrøder workshop ledger."/><meta property="og:image" content={heroImage.src}/><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/><title>Schrøder 1965 – Danish Reynolds 531 racer · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head>
<body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/schroder-1965/"/><main class="atala-page">
  <header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">In the collection · Copenhagen, Denmark · 1965</p><h1>Schrøder<br/><em>1965</em></h1><p>A Copenhagen-built Reynolds 531 racing bicycle. Its workshop ledger preserves what many old bicycles have lost: a frame number, a name and an exact date.</p></header>
  <figure class="quick-pass"><OptimizedImage src={heroImage} alt="Green 1965 Schrøder racing bicycle viewed from the drive side" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>The 1965 Schrøder from the drive side, with green paint, white lining and visible traces of use. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure>
  <section class="atala-facts" aria-label="Key facts"><div><strong>1965</strong><span>Delivered 22 May</span></div><div><strong>PS 1018 B</strong><span>Frame number</span></div><div><strong>Reynolds 531</strong><span>Lugged steel</span></div><div><strong>54 cm CT</strong><span>Stated frame size</span></div></section>
  <section class="atala-opening"><p class="section-label">A line in the workshop ledger</p><p>Beside frame number PS 1018 B are the name Gert V. Nilsen and the note “delivered 22/5/65”. The brief entry makes the bicycle more than an anonymous green racer: it connects the surviving frame with the person for whom it was originally built.</p></section>
  <section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">Copenhagen craftsmanship</p><h2>Built for one particular rider</h2></div><div><p>Schrøder Cykler was established by Peter Schrøder in Copenhagen in 1920. The workshop became known for hand-built road and track bicycles, made at a time when the framebuilder could adapt dimensions and handling to an individual customer.</p><p>This bicycle shows that tradition in physical form. It was built with Reynolds 531 tubing, Nervex lugs and Campagnolo dropouts. Its paint and lining carry marks from use, while the Schrøder name and workshop emblems remain clearly visible.</p></div></section>
  <section class="atala-details"><figure><OptimizedImage src={stemAndHeadset} alt="Stem, headset and cables on the Schrøder racing bicycle"/><figcaption>The stem, headset and external cables at the front of the bicycle.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={cableGuide} alt="Metal cable guide on the top tube of the green Schrøder racer"/><figcaption>A small chromed guide carries the cable along the top tube.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={headBadge} alt="Worn Copenhagen Schrøder head badge above the Balilla front brake"/><figcaption>The Copenhagen head badge above the Balilla front brake, both showing clear traces of use.</figcaption></figure></section>
  <section class="atala-components"><div><p class="section-label">The bicycle today</p><h2>Valentino and Balilla</h2></div><div><p>The bicycle is fitted with a <a href={`${base}/en/components/campagnolo-valentino/`}>Campagnolo Valentino gear group</a> and Balilla brakes. Valentino was Campagnolo’s simpler shifting system and suits a road racer on which dependable mechanics mattered more than a complete professional-level group.</p><p>The photographs document the present build. Without a complete original component list, we cannot establish that every part was fitted when the frame was delivered in 1965.</p></div></section>
  <section class="atala-detail-pair"><figure><OptimizedImage src={rearDerailleur} alt="Campagnolo Valentino rear derailleur and freewheel on the 1965 Schrøder"/><figcaption>The Campagnolo Valentino rear derailleur, freewheel and rear-facing cable loop.</figcaption></figure><div><p class="section-label">A Danish track connection</p><h2>Schrøder and Niels Fredborg</h2><p>The marque developed a strong connection with Danish track cycling. Schrøder’s later company history links Niels Fredborg’s 1,000-metre world title in 1967 and his Olympic silver in 1968 with Schrøder bicycles. Fredborg later won Olympic gold in 1972 and bronze in 1976.</p><p>Those results belong to the larger history of the marque. They do not make this road bicycle a Fredborg bicycle; the person documented for this frame is Gert V. Nilsen.</p></div></section>
  <section class="atala-details"><figure><OptimizedImage src={frontDerailleur} alt="Campagnolo Valentino front derailleur on the green Schrøder racer"/><figcaption>The Valentino front derailleur sits close to the chainset in the present build.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={downtubeShifter} alt="Campagnolo friction lever on the down tube of the 1965 Schrøder"/><figcaption>The Campagnolo friction lever sits on the down tube beside the Schrøder name.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={rearBrake} alt="Balilla rear brake and lining on the Schrøder racing bicycle"/><figcaption>The Balilla rear brake and surviving lining around the seat tube.</figcaption></figure></section>
  <section class="atala-detail-pair"><figure><img src={`${images}/workshop-ledger.jpg`} alt="Schrøder workshop ledger showing Gert V. Nilsen, frame PS 1018 B and delivery on 22 May 1965" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The workshop ledger links PS 1018 B with Gert V. Nilsen and records delivery on 22 May 1965. Source: private archive.</figcaption></figure><div><p class="section-label">Documented provenance</p><h2>Three details that survived</h2><p>Old racing bicycles often lose their first connection with workshop and owner. Here the identity of the frame can be followed directly in the surviving ledger: number, name and delivery date appear together on one line.</p><p>The note does not tell us how Gert V. Nilsen used the bicycle. We leave that gap open. What it does provide is a much firmer date than an assessment based only on paint, transfers and components.</p></div></section>
  <section class="koga-specs"><div><p class="section-label">The bicycle in the collection</p><h2>Brief specification</h2></div><dl><div><dt>Make and year</dt><dd>Schrøder, 1965</dd></div><div><dt>Frame number</dt><dd>PS 1018 B</dd></div><div><dt>Original customer</dt><dd>Gert V. Nilsen</dd></div><div><dt>Delivery</dt><dd>22 May 1965, according to the workshop ledger</dd></div><div><dt>Size</dt><dd>54 cm, centre to top</dd></div><div><dt>Frame</dt><dd>Reynolds 531 tubing, Nervex lugs and Campagnolo dropouts</dd></div><div><dt>Gears</dt><dd>Campagnolo Valentino, presently fitted</dd></div><div><dt>Brakes</dt><dd>Balilla, presently fitted</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>In the collection</dd></div></dl></section>
  <section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">Sources</p><h2>The basis of this page</h2></div><div><p>The frame number, name and delivery date come from the displayed page of the Schrøder workshop ledger. The frame, transfers and present components have been assessed from the bicycle and the collection photographs.</p><p>The workshop history was compared with the company history published by Schrøder Cykler/Adventure Cycling. Niels Fredborg’s results were checked against the DIF Museum and Aarhus Cyklebane.</p></div></section>
  <section class="atala-closing"><p>The most valuable thing about this Schrøder is not only the Reynolds tubing or profiled lugs. It is the small, unbroken connection between the workshop, the frame number and the first customer in May 1965.</p><div class="bike-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back to bicycles</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1960-1969/`}>View the period 1960–1969 →</a></div></section>
</main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
